#{[#edb/ref 17592186049731 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125322} :activity/id #uuid "7d6120fd-aa08-5562-9120-ce173e8fe311" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125322 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125752 :tutorial/content "A function is **invertible** on an interval if and only if it's one-to-one. In other words, it must pass the horizontal line test.\n\nNot all functions have inverses! For example, consider the function $f(x)=x^2-1$ whose graph is shown below. \n\n![](images/e9/e99832fa1867a8314dda20f392d0c897ebb2942ac91a652ab1cf474b5d2d9612.png)\n\nNotice that $f(x)$ is not a one-to-one since it does not satisfy the horizontal line test.\n\nTo find the graph of a function's inverse, we usually reflect it over the line $y=x.$ However, if we reflect $f(x)$ over the line $y=x,$ the result is not a function since it will not satisfy the vertical line test.\n\n![](images/7a/7a5a95f1cc3e2c8b40e865a24d22f4529e47c3c0137a6cb8d0369059f4a89a56.png)\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is *not* invertible.\n\nIf a function is not invertible, we can sometimes make it invertible by restricting its domain.\n\nHere, if we take $f(x)=x^2-1$ over the restricted interval $x \\in (0,\\infty),$ then it passes the horizontal line test and we can find the corresponding inverse function by reflecting the graph of $y=f(x)$ over the line $y=x.$\n\n![](images/0a/0a7f98c2dce4599ee74cc13dc53c3a693b9c3716ffe54c708811f4c011f18b6d.png)\n\nTo determine if a function is invertible over a particular domain, we need to use the horizontal line test to check if it's a one-to-one function." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125323}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125323 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124472 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124283 :question/id #uuid "454d081a-fdcb-5101-9f85-0e3b27986695" :question/math-academy-id "e-3961" :question/problem "Which of the graphs below correspond to an invertible function?\n\n![](images/6f/6fbded3e0a3faefda0e1f2c8418f5e271fb116cde4082487096378f89511b695.png)" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.  \n\n![](images/50/5036479a948abee5644cd3b96632f6aaf8ee370f7473ba27e11d25bb84ddb781.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that: \n\n- In graph IV, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graph IV  shows a one-to-one function. This means that the function is invertible.\n\n- For graphs I, II, and III, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. So, these are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only\"."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Invertible Function From Its Graph"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125324}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125324 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124473 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124284 :question/id #uuid "b6722faa-6069-59c1-8861-75b8207b0a90" :question/math-academy-id "e-3962" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d965e0b1087ad45206dd9259c963609f556bf5803af47ab18006bf40dffb2557.png)\n\n     \n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\n- $(-3,-1]$\n\n- $(0,4]$\n\n- $[-2,2)$" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.   \n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-3,-1].$  Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/24/245484a6a77160a5f5892738a7e1f8e488f6df8e4ba990bbc94ae964c3305525.png)\n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the intervals $(0,4]$ and $[-2,2)$ because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/47/4737a1460e1abd4edc200404407fef10761f038d9a29e52ba0f8c3e8c69bb93f.png)\n\n          \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I  only\"."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125325}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125325 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124474 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124285 :question/id #uuid "7c28a136-0b86-5d13-a5f8-b0dd0e0ea1ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-3963" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=|x-2|-2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x \\in (-\\infty, 2]$\n\n- $x \\in (0, \\infty)$\n\n- $x \\in (-1,4]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's graph the given function. The graph of the function $f(x)=|x-2|-2$ can be obtained in the following way:  \n\n- Take the curve $y=|x|.$\n\n- Translate it by $2$ units to the right,  to get $y= |x-2|.$\n\n- Finally, translate $y= |x-2|$ by $2$ units down to get $f(x)=|x-2|-2.$\n\n![](images/60/60c2601abfd3c4034991408f47b75c8cf70a1bccb72f73b2f9624a52d0ed7b42.png)\n\nNow, remember that a function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the interval $x\\in(-\\infty,2].$  Indeed, on this interval, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/45/45b0cdd26280d9379a449af41edbc9e69dd9d76f3b4116d938fbe708c21f6f29.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on any of the intervals $x\\in(0,\\infty)$ and $x \\in (-1,4].$ On these intervals, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/55/55b8483c59362d3a59dfd269db74f2c5f17a97cee1f25c896ece16890d80bd42.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"}}] :activity/title "Invertible Functions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124694}] [#edb/ref 17592186049762 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134937} :activity/id #uuid "e33ca737-353c-5d82-bb61-fdad7272cf57" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134927 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135161 :tutorial/content "To understand the following work, it's a good idea to review Green's theorem.\n\nSuppose $C$ is a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, and simple closed curve in $\\Bbb R^2,$ and $D$ is the region enclosed by $C.$ If we consider the two-dimensional vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\langle P, Q\\rangle$ to have zero $z$-component, i.e.,  \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = \\langle P,\\: Q,\\:0\\rangle\n$$\n\nand the scalar functions $P$ and $Q$ have continuous partial derivatives in the interior of $D,$ then Green's theorem can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{k} \\: \\textrm{d}A  = \\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$\n\n \n\nGreen's theorem relates the sum of the microscopic curls of $\\mathbf{F}$ over $D$ to the macroscopic curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ (i.e., the circulation) along the boundary of $D.$\n\n![](images/78/7837a682bfbf9d39ab03e42edee33306b53bf66be2fa11f91d2cdfa62ca9e9ac.png)\n\nGreen's theorem is a special case of another important theorem, which we'll discuss shortly. But first, we need to look at the relationship between oriented surfaces and their bounding curves." :tutorial/title "A Review of Green's Theorem"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134938}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134928 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133926 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133486 :question/id #uuid "4f12e12d-37b3-5c41-9284-bc369ccd4c0f" :question/math-academy-id "e-12421" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = y \\,\\mathbf{i} -x \\,\\mathbf{j} + z\\,\\mathbf{k}$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t) =  \\sin (\\pi t)\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\cos (\\pi t)\\, \\mathbf{j} + t(2-t)\\,\\mathbf{k}$ for $t \\in [0,2].$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $\\Gamma$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r\n= \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = \\sin (\\pi t), \\qquad\ny = \\cos (\\pi t), \\qquad\nz = t(2-t) = 2t-t^2.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i-x\\,j+z\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+(2t-{t}^{2})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r'(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+2(1-t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+(2t-{t}^{2})\\,k\\,)\\cdot (\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+2(1-t)\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))+(2t-{t}^{2})\\cdot 2(1-t) \\\\  & =\\pi {cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)+2(2t-{t}^{2})(1-t) \\\\  & =\\pi ({cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t))+2(2t-3{t}^{2}+{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\pi +4t-6{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\pi +4t-6{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\pi t+2{t}^{2}-2{t}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[2\\pi +8-16+8]-[0] \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Circulation Over Space Curves"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134927}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134929 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133927 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133487 :question/id #uuid "87c9cf21-797c-5893-a81b-580685331739" :question/math-academy-id "e-12422" :question/problem "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} = \\langle -xz, \\: -yz, \\: x+z^2 \\rangle,$ and $C$ is the intersection of the paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2$ and the plane $z=1.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n= \\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2$ and the plane $z=1.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz = g(x,y) = 1.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-xz,\\,-yz,\\,{z}^{2}+x⟩{|}_{z=1} \\\\  & =⟨{{-x}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{-y}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{1+x}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(-x)\\cdot 0-(-y)\\cdot 0+1+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1+x)\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNow, note that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=1$ of radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r \\sin\\theta, \\qquad\n\\textrm{d}A = r \\, \\textrm{d}r \\textrm{d}\\theta,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1+x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }r+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[r\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\pi r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\pi [{r}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral Using Stokes' Theorem"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134930 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133928 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133488 :question/id #uuid "f3f9694a-14f8-54c3-be32-744c2dd51736" :question/math-academy-id "e-12435" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t) = (t^2-t^3)\\,\\mathbf{i} + (t^2-t) \\, \\mathbf{j} + (t^2-t)\\,\\mathbf{k}$ for $t \\in [0,1).$\nGiven that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\mathbf{i} + x\\, \\mathbf{j} + y\\,\\mathbf{k},$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n= \\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = t^2-t^3, \\qquad\ny = t^2-t, \\qquad\nz = t^2-t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =i+x\\,j+y\\,k \\\\  & =i+({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\,j+({t}^{2}-t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r'(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,k \\\\  & =(2t-3{t}^{2})\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j+(2t-1)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(i+({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\,j+({t}^{2}-t)\\,k)\\cdot ((2t-3{t}^{2})\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j+(2t-1)\\,k) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (2t-3{t}^{2})+({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\cdot (2t-1)+({t}^{2}-t)\\cdot (2t-1) \\\\  & =2t-3{t}^{2}+(-{t}^{3}+2{t}^{2}-t)\\cdot (2t-1) \\\\  & =2t-3{t}^{2}-2{t}^{4}+{t}^{3}+4{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}-2{t}^{2}+t \\\\  & =3t-7{t}^{2}+5{t}^{3}-2{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3t-7{t}^{2}+5{t}^{3}-2{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{7}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{4}-\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Surface Integral Using Stokes' Theorem"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134931}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134931 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135162 :tutorial/content "Calculating the circulation of a vector field along a closed, piecewise smooth curve $C$ can be time-consuming because it often requires splitting $C$ into several parts, evaluating a line integral along each one, and calculating their sum. \n\nHowever, in such cases, we can often use Stokes' theorem to reduce the amount of work required by converting this sum of line integrals to a single surface integral. \n\nFor example, suppose that $C$ is the intersection of the paraboloid $z=x^2 + y^2$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x = \\pm 1$ and $y=\\pm 1.$ Suppose also that $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above, and \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = \\bigg\\langle 0, \\: \\dfrac{1}{2}z^2, \\: xz \\bigg\\rangle.\n$$\n\nLet's use Stokes' theorem to find the circulation of $\\mathbf{F}$ along $C$.\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n= \\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy computing the curl of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n![](images/fd/fd25c80fd757477b724b320d1941c57630ec1c64f36bdb44d4c1c70c526fa2a9.svg)\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-z,\\,-z,\\,0⟩{|}_{z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨{{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr & ={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot 2x+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot 2y+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2({x}^{3}+{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/dc/dcf7ba14ac2c0f3da12efed6d539817ab1708273f5e8e665fdde14fee28bb4e3.png)\n\nEvaluating this integral using the usual methods, we get\n\n$$\n2\\int_{-1}^{1} \\int_{-1}^{1} x^3 + x^2 y + x y^2 + y^3 \\: \\textrm{d}y  \\: \\textrm{d}x = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = 0.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Stokes theorem tells us that \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\n depends only on the circulation of $\\mathbf F$ at the boundary of $S.$ Therefore, we could have evaluated our line integral by selecting *any* surface with $C$ as its bounding curve and integrating $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ over this surface." :tutorial/title "Using Stokes' Theorem to Evaluate Line Integrals"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134929}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134932 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135163 :tutorial/content "We're now ready to state the main result of the lesson: \n\n*Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then* \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}.\n$$\n\nThis result is known as **Stokes' theorem** and can be considered a generalization of Green's theorem. Let's spend some time building some intuition behind this result.\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field in $\\mathbb R^3.$ Stokes' theorem states that the sum of the microscopic curls of $\\mathbf F$ on the surface $S$ equals the macroscopic curl of $\\mathbf F$ at the boundary $C$ of $S.$ \n\n![](images/30/308aec6213f8dd42c4f853078c0305901bc19568bfd5132221bd72743f4aa58a.png)\n\nConsider the left-hand side of the above picture, and suppose we have a point $P$ that lies on $S.$ \n\nThe magnitude of the flux of $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ through $S$ at $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf n.\n$$\n\nWe can think of this quantity as the amount of \"swirl\" of $\\mathbf F$ at $P$ that acts tangentially to $S$ at $P.$\n\nIf we sum (integrate) the flux of the curl through $S$ at every point over the entire surface $S,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf n\\, \\textrm{d}S\n$$\n\nwhich we usually write as\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nThis expression gives us the left-hand side of Stokes' theorem.\n\nLet's now look at the right-hand side of our image. The macroscopic curl is simply the circulation of $\\mathbf F$ along the boundary of $S{:}$\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r\n$$\n\nEquating the sum of the microscopic curls with the circulation, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\oint\\limits_C\\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r.\n$$\n\nStokes' theorem can be summarized in a single picture as follows:\n\n![](images/34/34b39be1166c0556eaad00a473684916c0725c186829f6085db1a322da29612a.png)\n\nWe can immediately deduce a remarkable fact. Notice the value of the surface integral depends only on the circulation at the boundary. Therefore, within a given vector field, if two surfaces $S_1$ and $S_2$ have the same boundary $C,$ then their respective surface integrals will be equal!\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S_1} \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_{S_2} \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Stokes' Theorem"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134930}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134937 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135168 :tutorial/content "The **circulation** $\\Gamma$ of a three-dimensional vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$ along a closed, piecewise-smooth curve $C \\in \\mathbb R^3$ parametrized by $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r = \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThis is the same definition as the two-dimensional case. Also, like the two-dimensional case, $\\Gamma$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along a closed curve $C,$ traversed once.\n\nRemember that circulation is always measured with respect to our chosen orientation for $C$ (positive or negative).\n\nLet's get some practice at computing circulation over three-dimensional curves." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134928}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134938 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135169 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have an oriented surface $S$ (not closed) whose **boundary curve** is the closed curve $C,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/bc/bc0ac6b400535839d3571d61c07f3ac752bc4e85020f9b3e4cc08410b0023822.png)\n\nWhenever we select an orientation for $S,$ our chosen orientation automatically induces a positive orientation for $C.$\n\nFor example, if we select the upward-pointing unit normal vector shown below, the positive orientation is *counterclockwise* when viewed from above.\n\n![](images/73/7353d1a742e374e3704155e8c99cc26c62e8486f3606cba1144c35871ad3c432.png)\n\nAssume that you are standing on the curve $C$ with your head pointing upward (i.e., in the same direction as $\\mathbf n$). Then, if you traverse $C$ in the positive direction, the surface will always be on your left.\n\nFor example, if $z=f(x,y)$ is upward-oriented, the projection of the bounding curve onto the $xy$-plane runs counterclockwise when viewed from above. \n\n![](images/e9/e948c13c040a01ce46b6f715e313c8aabb2634e5871739169dd3f38e3229e768.png)\n\nNow, suppose we select the opposite orientation for $\\mathbf n,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/81/81d5e3126c07f30b68bd2d46c4104212f6c7edf34baf65b077ad297f224ebe8a.png)\n\nBy choosing the *opposite* orientation for $\\mathbf n,$ the positive orientation for $C$ is *clockwise* when viewed from above!\n\nIf you're not convinced, grab a child's doll, a toothbrush, or any other object with some kind of head, and mark its left and right sides (be careful not to damage it!). Then, walk it around the edge of a table so that its left side is always next to the table surface. If you turn it upside down, you will see that the object must traverse the table's edge in the *opposite* direction for the table surface to stay on the left." :tutorial/title "Induced Orientations of Boundary Curves"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134932}}] :activity/title "Stokes' Theorem" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134013}] [#edb/ref 17592186049791 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185033} :activity/id #uuid "a2477a32-3a3d-5c39-9c58-768e2c028225" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184899 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184148 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184055 :question/id #uuid "fdd0c8ab-6977-5a8f-b1db-37ae9b2b116e" :question/math-academy-id "e-3663" :question/problem "The graph below shows the function $y=\\cot{(Bx)}+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What are the values of $B$ and $D?$\n\n![](images/06/062083968cd64f10523e99e2dd4aecb405f28f781b8b0c194f11b6017943a801.png)" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is $\\dfrac \\pi 2.$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\cot(Bx + C) + D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{\\pi}{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac \\pi 2  = \\dfrac{\\pi}{B}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad B = \\dfrac{\\pi}{\\left(\\dfrac \\pi 2\\right)} = 2.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the tangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $1$ unit down. Therefore, $D=-1.$\n\nSo, the final equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\cot\\left(2x\\right)-1.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Stretch and Vertical Shift of a Transformed Cotangent Curve"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184900}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184900 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184149 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184056 :question/id #uuid "90986bdd-15dd-51c6-b5b0-4174542736c9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3669" :question/problem "![](images/5a/5a58c1b6b72ab29c73457834bd093b23df234d4b161d87b026449ce18a510cb6.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function ![](images/25/2590787ae9eed509fa2eca140078f34dc0263e5d888cb50ef81bb317896f4fa7.png) where ![](images/4a/4a4b94629aa74d8b04caecd666b2b34f6d7a4db03b561a0207102093f3ce0366.png) is a constant. Given that ![](images/f7/f75fd972834cbdd20e3329eee1406c25476109f66bdc63b50e65d43b495416c8.png) what is the value of ![](images/05/0514151a4defc8b3e97ec86ead73038697701ead4a5999db5c16affffd20c41f.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parameter ![](images/04/04d3d028f9f3766f1e44fb4a095a62dc92a9f16259ac8ee930254e2da0e52ae0.png) represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n![](images/23/23f74abe2b793b6e5d090136cafe331c5f92d4ff62df66ec94b75539259b5a6e.png)\n\nThe factor ![](images/25/2500722483533ba6dd622e9572f36ef0162889a6801352ce8f2c60f60c55167e.png) indicates that the graph of ![](images/dd/dd80268d579d79a6f0ca99485ec391b4034877f1f14a52fa9e6cd371cf2bfaee.png) is a horizontal translation of ![](images/f3/f324f716f57c696de3669f03ba36b1aa197bed1c36144777252490084c940344.png) Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/4a/4ae5b54c0b0aafdb3e191dd01d0c3ff698bda9daee3a51ad49ecf0a0313c8186.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting ![](images/a8/a878f9eac36b11ec0a6ac3ba961c0257a9e73e872127c0167d9b64554acc84b6.png) by ![](images/b7/b7bbd8a714eb95c5081e7511b5b003ead8d98e0d38de8dc061d82ac15a486752.png) units to the right. Therefore,\n\n![](images/3b/3bcb5d5f6e6988266fedfb0cc507aa46fdbf510e2d15ca8588253228af091621.png)\n\nSince ![](images/9c/9c5ec1702df206d7f81e68ea6fbc5b795335b0b9317a4540446fe5c4235aa0bc.png) and ![](images/65/65f6e2f05e1d05f16a4f6601ebe3e49f4cb01663d31807feb5de5f6bb9e2331e.png) is in this interval, the value of ![](images/b5/b5c9da4fbe9c1486e2080416f6a3044a790267f9eb072ef34c164e5e12958e5c.png) is ![](images/ee/eef2c2e86e460a67d52eee56af496164737b41a0e2db4c1e6d4a4030b4c00167.png)."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Shift of a Horizontally Stretched Tangent or Cotangent Curve"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185033 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185353 :tutorial/content "We can apply combinations of vertical and horizontal shifts and stretches to tangent and cotangent curves.\n\nFor example, let's consider the function\n\n$$\ny = \\tan(Bx) + D,\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ and $D$ are constants. The graph of this function is shown below.\n\n![](images/58/58c3a7647037c409235c0bf0c532794e0bc8eec47daf379c23488b0a0c8bac42.png)\n\nLet's compute the values of $B$ and $D$ using our knowledge of graph transformations.\n\nFirstly, recall that we can calculate the period $T$ of a tangent or cotangent function by considering the difference in the $x$-values between two consecutive asymptotes. Therefore, \n\n$$\nT =  \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} - \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y = A\\tan(Bx + C) + D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{\\pi}{B}.\n$$\n\nBy applying this formula, we can calculate the value of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{3} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{B} \\quad\\Longrightarrow \\quad B = \\dfrac{\\pi}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)} = 3\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the tangent function. The graph shows that the function has been translated by $1$ unit down. Hence, $D = -1.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of our curve is\n\n$$\ny = \\tan(3x) - 1.\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185034}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185034 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184236 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183918 :question/id #uuid "0431c02c-9a72-5d5b-ad75-550f413b74f1" :question/math-academy-id "e-10025" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5c84e8bf0c80a5c57252ff368d0f59499b0299d4e59f49fdf738e3869f0bcf4f.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y = \\tan(Bx) + D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B + D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT =  \\dfrac{\\pi}{5} - \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\right) = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y = A\\tan(Bx + C) + D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{\\pi}{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{B} \\quad\\Longrightarrow \\quad B = \\dfrac{\\pi}{\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}\\right)} = \\dfrac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the tangent function. The graph shows that the function has been translated by $1$ unit up. Hence, $D = 1.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nB + D =\\dfrac{5}{2} +1 = \\dfrac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny = \\tan\\left(\\frac{5x}{2} \\right) +1.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Stretch and Vertical Shift of a Transformed Tangent Curve"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184899}}] :activity/title "Combining Graph Transformations of Tangent and Cotangent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186184434}] [#edb/ref 17592186049774 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230082} :activity/id #uuid "51694459-f27a-580f-878c-371665540655" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230082 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230205 :tutorial/content "In the previous lesson, we focused on dilution problems in which the incoming solution contained none of the substance being modeled. In those cases, the rate at which the substance entered the container was zero.\n\nIn many practical situations, however, the incoming solution *does* contain some amount of the substance. When this happens,\n\n- the rate of entry is no longer zero, and\n- the resulting differential equation takes the form of a first-order linear differential equation.\n\nFor example, suppose a storage tank initially holds $650\\,\\textrm{L}$ of water containing $1\\,300\\,\\textrm{g}$ of dissolved chlorine.\n\n- A solution containing $20\\,\\textrm{g/L}$ of dissolved chlorine is pumped into the tank at a rate of $5\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\n- At the same time, the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $5\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\n\nTo construct the model for the amount of chlorine $Q$ in the tank, we compare the rates of chlorine entering and leaving. The derivative $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}$ describes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which chlorine enters the tank minus the rate at which chlorine leaves the tank.\n\n$$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}}$$\n\nWe compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which chlorine enters the tank.\n\nThe rate of chlorine entering is the concentration times the flow rate: $$\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{'} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (5\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\ & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (5\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\ & =100\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}\\end{aligned}$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which chlorine exits the tank.\n\nSince the volume remains constant at $650\\,\\textrm{L},$ the concentration in the tank is $\\dfrac{Q}{650}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{L}}.$ So, the rate of chlorine exiting is $$\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{'} & =(5\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{650}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\ & =(5\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{650}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\ & =\\frac{Q}{130}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe net rate of change $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_\\text{in} - Q'_\\text{out}$ is the difference between the rate in and the rate out. Combining this with the initial condition $Q(0) = 1\\,300\\,\\textrm{g},$ we can state the complete initial value problem: $$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 100 - \\dfrac{1}{130}Q \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{1}{130}Q = 100, \\quad Q(0) = 1\\,300\\,\\textrm{g}}$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230083}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230083 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229181 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228949 :question/id #uuid "6b62d830-be21-5734-8a12-115b6a5136bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-21880" :question/problem "A fertilizer tank initially holds $800\\,\\textrm{L}$ of liquid containing $7\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved nutrient.\nA solution containing $0.01\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of dissolved nutrient is pumped into the tank at a rate of $40\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $40\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of nutrient $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of nutrient in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which nutrient enters the tank minus the rate at which nutrient leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.01\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of the nutrient enters the tank at a rate of $40\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *nutrient* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.01\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (40\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.01\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (40\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $800\\,\\textrm{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of nutrient in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{800}\\,\\textrm{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $40\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ *nutrient* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(40\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{800}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(40\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{800}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{40Q}{800}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{20}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.4-\\frac{Q}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{1}{20}Q = 0.4.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $Q_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{1}{20}Q_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nQ_c(t) = Ae^{-t/20},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nQ_p(t) = \\alpha.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $Q_p = \\alpha$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q_p}{\\mathrm{d}t} = (\\alpha)' = 0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0 + \\dfrac{1}{20}\\alpha = 0.4\n  \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n  \\alpha = 8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is $Q_p(t) = 8.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t) = Q_c(t) + Q_p(t) = Ae^{-t/20} + 8.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $7\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved nutrient. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0) = 7,$ and solve for $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =7 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/20}+8 & =7 \\\\ A & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\nQ(t) = 8 - e^{-t/20}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Dilution Model: Inflow Equals Outflow"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230084}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230084 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229182 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228930 :question/id #uuid "2f5d8d59-2cc7-53bf-92ad-6062df331851" :question/math-academy-id "e-21765" :question/problem "A water-treatment holding tank initially holds $700\\,\\textrm{L}$ of liquid containing $12\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved coagulant.\nA feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of the coagulant is pumped into the tank at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $20\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of coagulant in the tank $8$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of coagulant in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which coagulant enters the tank minus the rate at which coagulant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which coagulant enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of the coagulant enters the tank at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *coagulant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which coagulant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which coagulant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $700\\,\\textrm{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $30\\,\\textrm{L/min}$ and leaves at $20\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n700 + 30t - 20t = (700+10t)\\,\\textrm{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of coagulant in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{700+10t}\\,\\textrm{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ *coagulant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{700+10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{700+10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{700+10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{70+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =1.5-\\frac{2Q}{70+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{70+t}Q=1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(70+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|70+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(70+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(70+t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t) = (70+t)^2$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(70+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{70+t}Q) & =(70+t)^{2}\\cdot 1.5 \\\\ (70+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(70+t)Q & =1.5(70+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(70+t)^{2}) & =1.5(70+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(70+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 1.5(70+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(70+t)^{2} & =1.5\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(70+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.5(70+t)+\\frac{C}{(70+t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $12\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of coagulant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0) = 12,$ and solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =12 \\\\ 0.5(70+0)+\\frac{C}{(70+0)^{2}} & =12 \\\\ 35+\\frac{C}{{70}^{2}} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{70}^{2}} & =-23 \\\\ C & =-23(70)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.5(70+t)-\\frac{23(70)^{2}}{(70+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.5(70+t)-23{(\\frac{70}{70+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of coagulant in the tank $8$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(8) & =0.5(70+8)-23{(\\frac{70}{70+8})}^{2} \\\\  & =39-23{(\\frac{70}{78})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈20.48\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Dilution Model"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230085}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230085 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229183 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228931 :question/id #uuid "bbf602f0-1101-55d5-a6b3-9bfc5731e9ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-21766" :question/problem "A syrup mixing tank in a confectionery factory can hold up to $240\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $96\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of syrup solution with $32\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of dissolved sugar. A more concentrated syrup with concentration $1.5\\,\\textrm{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\textrm{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $16\\,\\textrm{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of sugar in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the tank minus the rate at which sugar leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.5\\,\\textrm{lb/gal}$ of sugar enters the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\textrm{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =36\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which sugar leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $96\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $24\\,\\textrm{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $16\\,\\textrm{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t) = 96 + 24t - 16t = 8(12+t)\\,\\textrm{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{8(12 + t)}\\,\\textrm{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $16\\,\\textrm{gal/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(16\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{8(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{8(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16Q}{8(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{12+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =36-\\frac{2Q}{12+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{2}{12 + t}\\,Q = 36.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(12+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|12+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(12+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t) = (12+t)^2$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(12+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{12+t}\\,Q) & =(12+t)^{2}\\cdot 36 \\\\ (12+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(12+t)Q & =36(12+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(12+t)^{2}) & =36(12+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(12+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 36(12+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(12+t)^{2} & =36\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(12+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =12(12+t)+\\frac{C}{(12+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $32\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of sugar. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0) = 32,$ and solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =32 \\\\ 12(12+0)+\\frac{C}{(12+0)^{2}} & =32 \\\\ 12\\cdot 12+\\frac{C}{(12)^{2}} & =32 \\\\ 144+\\frac{C}{{12}^{2}} & =32 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{12}^{2}} & =-112 \\\\ C & =-112(12)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =12(12+t)-\\frac{112(12)^{2}}{(12+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =12(12+t)-112{(\\frac{12}{12+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $t_o$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $240\\,\\textrm{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V(t_o) = 240$ to solve for $t_o{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =240 \\\\ 96+24{t}_{o}-16{t}_{o} & =240 \\\\ 96+8{t}_{o} & =240 \\\\ 8{t}_{o} & =144 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =18\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of sugar in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(18) & =12(12+18)-112{(\\frac{12}{12+18})}^{2} \\\\  & =360-112{(\\frac{12}{30})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈342.1\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Amount at an Overflow Point"}}] :activity/title "Modeling Mixture Problems With First-Order Linear ODEs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186229261}]}
